From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The LineageOS Project <noreply@lineageos.org>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] keymaster: no-op delete ops when the legacy HAL omits them

The stock MediaTek/Trustonic keymaster 2.0 module (libMcTeeKeymaster.so)
implements generate/import/begin/update/finish but has no delete_key or
delete_all_keys ops. Keymaster2PassthroughContext calls those null pointers
unconditionally, aborting android.hardware.keymaster@3.0-service during
fingerprint enrollment. Keystore then returns a null credential response and
Settings crashes.

Return KM_ERROR_OK when the legacy device has no delete op. This replaces the
earlier "disable CFI" workaround: the CFI abort was itself caused by the null
indirect-call target, so once the null call is skipped CFI can remain enabled.
---
 contexts/keymaster2_passthrough_context.cpp | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/contexts/keymaster2_passthrough_context.cpp b/contexts/keymaster2_passthrough_context.cpp
index 678eaaa..e579745 100644
--- a/contexts/keymaster2_passthrough_context.cpp
+++ b/contexts/keymaster2_passthrough_context.cpp
@@ -107,10 +107,15 @@ keymaster_error_t Keymaster2PassthroughContext::ParseKeyBlob(const KeymasterKeyB
 }
 
 keymaster_error_t Keymaster2PassthroughContext::DeleteKey(const KeymasterKeyBlob& blob) const {
+    // The stock MediaTek/Trustonic keymaster 2.0 module does not implement
+    // delete_key; calling the null ops pointer aborts the HAL. Key blobs are
+    // managed by keystore, so treating deletion as a no-op is safe here.
+    if (!device_->delete_key) return KM_ERROR_OK;
     return device_->delete_key(device_, &blob);
 }
 
 keymaster_error_t Keymaster2PassthroughContext::DeleteAllKeys() const {
+    if (!device_->delete_all_keys) return KM_ERROR_OK;
     return device_->delete_all_keys(device_);
 }
 
